t-of-bounds could both apply prefer observable sink type; list at most one CWE unless multiple clearly distinct sink types (different branches) visible. For control-flow: verify validation before dangerous operation, verify break/return/continue terminates dangerous path, verify loop bounds against array sizes with quoted snippet pairs, verify integer arithmetic uses correct operators and checks underflow/overflow/unsigned wrap — state explicitly when guard checks one variable (authkey flags) but sink uses another (rka derived from payload.data[0]). For localization: snippet must contain sink line(s); include exact dereference lines; use ≤3 line ranges unless source and sink span more. Analysis priorities in sequence: (1) follow actual data-flow from input sources (arguments, parsed fields, external/network/file input) to sinks; quote snippet at each transition and state attacker-controlled; (2) check integer arithmetic for indexing (off-by-one > vs >=, overflow, underflow, unsigned wrap) against array bounds; (3) check missing/misplaced null/validity checks BEFORE dereference of pointers derived from untrusted input, verify guard applies to same pointer/field at sink; (4) check authorization/authentication order relative to dangerous actions (guard must precede sink and apply to sink's object); (5) check format-string, command-injection, HTML-output injection where user data reaches sink directly; (6) check missing/null payload/array/data validation before indexing — if payload.data, array base, or derived pointer indexed without prior null/non-empty validation treat as unvalidated access. Key failure-pattern checks to apply within each SCoT step: when pointer assigned from structured field (authkey->payload.data[0], ptr = obj->field) verify whether array/field itself validated before indexing — if not, assignment is unvalidated even if parent pointer (authkey) checked; when guard only checks parent object but sink dereferences derived pointer, state mismatch expli